      "problem": "Calculate $\\int \\frac{4}{{z}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z.$",
      "worked_solution": "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{{z}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z & =4\\int {z}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{{z}^{-4+1}}{-4+1})+C \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{{z}^{-3}}{-3})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {z}^{-3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3{z}^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
